## CLI surface, probed READ-ONLY with `qmark` first

```
awplus(config-if)# auth dynamic-vlan-creation ?
  rule  VLAN assignment rule
  type  VLAN assignment type
  <cr>
awplus(config-if)# auth dynamic-vlan-creation type ?
  multi   Multiple Dynamic vlan VLAN
  single  Single Dynamic VLAN
awplus(config-if)# auth dynamic-vlan-creation type multi rule ?
  deny    Deny different assigned vlan id
  permit  Permit different assigned vlan id
awplus(config-if)# auth multi-vlan-session ?
  <cr>
awplus(config-radsrv)# user ckdv1 password ckdv1pw ?
  group   Specify the group for user
  reject  Reject authentication for user
  <cr>
awplus(config-radsrv-group)# vlan ?
  WORD  VLAN information string
```

🔴 **The local RADIUS server has no per-user `vlan` keyword.** The VLAN is carried by a **group**:
`group <name>` then `vlan <id>`, and the user is put in the group. `show radius local-server
user` has a `Vlan` column that is populated through the group, and `show radius local-server
group` lists `Vlan`, `Egress-VLANID` and `Egress-VLAN-Name`.

## The rig this case had to build, and what it changed

Group A's rig has no VLAN-carrying users and no VLANs other than the campaign's own. Under the
Test Engineer's standing instruction that device configuration is the tester's, this case
**created and then removed**:

| object | what | why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `vlan 362` `ckDYNVLAN-A`, `interface vlan362` `10.38.100.1/24` | a dynamic-assignment target with an L3 address | so a supplicant placed in it has something to ARP for |
| `vlan 363` `ckDYNVLAN-B`, `interface vlan363` `10.38.101.1/24` | the second target | so the two supplicants can be told apart in the dataplane |
| `radius-server local` group `ckdvg1` `vlan 362`, group `ckdvg2` `vlan 363` | the VLAN attribute source | the only way the local server can return a VLAN |
| users `ckdv1` (group ckdvg1) and `ckdv2` (group ckdvg2) | the two supplicants' identities | |

```
show radius local-server user
User-Name                                     Group             Vlan
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ckdv1                                         ckdvg1            362
ckdv2                                         ckdvg2            363
ckmulti1 / ckmulti2 / ckpeap / cktls          (group A's four, untouched)

show radius local-server group
Group-Name       Vlan             Egress-VLANID  Egress-VLAN-Name
---------------------------------------------------------------------
ckdvg1           362              -              -
ckdvg2           363              -              -
```

**Everything in that table was removed at teardown and the removal is verified below.** The two
new VLANs were chosen in unused subnets (10.38.100.0/24, 10.38.101.0/24); **vlan 60, vlan 10 and
vlan 48 were not touched**, and the spanning tree was re-read afterwards and is unchanged.

### The two supplicants

| name | MAC | RADIUS identity | group -> VLAN | sender IP used in its own VLAN |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **S1** | `02:0d:b9:00:33:62` | `ckdv1` | ckdvg1 -> **362** | 10.38.100.50 |
| **S2** | `02:0d:b9:00:33:63` | `ckdv2` | ckdvg2 -> **363** | 10.38.101.50 |

The dataplane probe is 3 ARP requests for a VLAN's gateway address, **sent with a sender IP
inside that same subnet**, so the only thing that differs between a reaching leg and a blocked
one is **which VLAN the untagged frame landed in**. The measurement is how many ARP replies
come back.

🔴 **Host-mode.** Two supplicants on one port need `auth host-mode multi-supplicant`, so this
case trips the campaign's standing finding and it did, exactly as documented: `show running-config
interface port1.0.48` read `dot1x control-direction both` before the command and **`dot1x
control-direction in` after it**, with no warning and no `% ` line. **Not a new observation --
same trigger, same rewrite, same silence as T33360 and T33354.** `dot1x control-direction both`
was restored explicitly at teardown and read back.

Every leg was preceded by `dot1x initialize interface port1.0.48`.

